<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tale of Sinuhe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 19th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century-bce">19th century BCE</a> · Also known as Story of Sinuhe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tale of Sinuhe is an ancient Egyptian literary work of the Twelfth Dynasty, often regarded as the finest piece of Middle Egyptian literature. It tells of the courtier Sinuhe, who flees to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> on hearing of the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a>, prospers there among foreign chiefs and returns home in old age to be pardoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> and buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Many copies survive on papyri and ostraca.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tale-of-sinuhe.jpg" alt="Tale of Sinuhe" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ancient Egyptian scribe; facsimile by Georg Möller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sinuhe-Papyrus_(Papyrus_Berlin_3022).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The story is known by several names, among them the Story of Sanehat. It was likely composed at the beginning of the Twelfth Dynasty, after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a> and the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> as sole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>.</p><p>The oldest known copy dates from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>, and the work was so popular within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> that newer copies have been found ranging up to 750 years after the original. It is known from more primary sources than any other ancient Egyptian literary narrative, at least five papyri and 30 ostraca, spanning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. Most other such narratives are known from a single period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The text opens in the style of the autobiographical inscriptions common in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> tombs, listing the titles and services of Sinuhe, a follower of the royal family. It then turns to his receiving news of the king's sudden death, apparently by assassination, while he is on an expedition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> with the king's eldest son. Fearing that he may be implicated in a conspiracy, Sinuhe flees, crosses Lake Maaty and nearly dies of thirst before a leader who once visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> recognises him and gives him water and boiled milk.</p><p>He meets Amunenshi, ruler of upper Retjenu, who entrusts him with command of his armies. He defeats a challenger, yet longs for home, and prays to be buried in the land where he was born. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> sends for him, and a long exchange of letters follows. The king offers him burial in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Sinuhe returns to court, where he is cleansed and restored to his Egyptian identity. The story closes in the form of a tomb inscription addressed to visitors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Modern readers have offered several reasons for his flight. The more common view, called by Meltzer the impulsive school, holds that he fled in a sudden fear of the disorder that followed the king's death. Others suggest that he was involved in the killing, or took the chance to escape the monarchy, and John Baines notes an interpretation in which guilt at abandoning his homeland drives his exile.</p><p>The name Sinuhe derives from the Egyptian for son of the sycamore, a tree associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>. The tale has been compared with the biblical story of Joseph, and its challenger with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goliath">Goliath</a>. It inspired the novel Sinuhe the Egyptian (1945) by Mika Waltari, which was made into a film.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Story of Sinuhe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Tale of Sinuhe</b>, the story of a courtier who flees to the Levant after the death of Amenemhat I and returns home in old age, the Tale of the Eloquent Peasant, the Tale of the Shipwrecked Sailor and the Dialogue of a Man and his Ba are…</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Middle Kingdom produced the classics of Egyptian literature, copied in schools for centuries: the <b>Story of Sinuhe</b>, an official who flees Egypt and returns in old age, often regarded as its masterpiece; the Tale of the Shipwrecked Sailor; the Eloquent Peasant; the dialogue of a man weary…</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
